% references.bib
%
% Every entry here has been checked against an external index, and the check is
% recorded in `CITATION_AUDIT.csv` (classical sources) and
% `AI_LITERATURE_CANDIDATES.md` (2023-2025 literature). Four rules governed what
% was allowed in:
%
%   1. No field is written from memory when an API returned a value for it.
%      Where memory and the API disagreed the API won, and the disagreement is
%      recorded in the audit file. The `author` field of madaan2023selfrefine is
%      the case in point: recall had a different last author entirely.
%   2. A work whose coordinates could not be verified gets no entry. Lyapunov
%      1892 is named in the prose as a classical ingredient and deliberately has
%      no entry: Crossref, OpenAlex and Semantic Scholar return only later
%      reprints and commentaries, so a volume and page range would have been
%      invented.
%   3. arXiv-only entries use the misc type with eprint fields. Their conference
%      placement (NeurIPS, ICLR) is widely reported but was not verified against
%      proceedings metadata, and an unverified booktitle is the exact failure
%      this file exists to avoid.
%   4. Explanatory text lives in these comments, never in a `note` field. A note
%      is typeset into the bibliography, which would put internal audit
%      commentary in front of a reviewer.
%
% Bibliographic notes, for the record:
%
%   atkinson1968 -- single author per Crossref, PubMed (PMID 4972613) and
%     OpenAlex. The repository's older index listed "Atkinson & Walton,
%     J. Biol. Chem.", which conflates this paper with Atkinson & Walton,
%     J. Biol. Chem. 240:757-763 (1965), on E. coli phosphofructokinase.
%
%   johnson2011michaelis -- cited instead of Michaelis & Menten 1913
%     (Biochem. Z. 49:333-369), whose coordinates no available index confirms.
%     This is the DOI-resolvable English translation of the same paper.
%
%   vanderpol1926 -- title as printed; Crossref stores it behind the article
%     number "LXXXVIII.", which is the journal's numbering, not the title.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Classical sources
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

